t64? np.argsort returns int64 → 406MB). Peak ~1.5GB. Probably OK.\n\n- To look up seeds: np.searchsorted into sorted hash array → candidate positions. For each read, take seeds at offsets [0, 62, 125] (k=25), i.e., three seeds spread across the read.\n\n- Candidates from seeds: cluster positions; for each candidate cluster (within read length), verify full-length match. Verification: compare the 2-bit codes of read and reference over 150 positions. Vectorize across candidates: gather reference window codes (len 150 × num candidates). Per read, num candidates is small (usually 1-3). Total 664k reads × ~2 candidates × 150 → 200M byte comparisons, fine with numpy (each read processed individually in Python loop → 664k iterations might be slow in pure Python... each iteration does small numpy ops with overhead ~10-20µs → 10-15s. OK).\n\nActually, simpler verification: since reads are error-free, if a seed hits, just check that the full 150-mer hash exists? Compute full-length 150-mer hash — doesn't fit in 64 bits. Alternative: two k-mer seeds at both ends (offsets 0 and 125, k=25): if both hit at consistent positions (pos2 = pos1 + 125), the full read matches with very high probability. But for reads containing errors, both ends might fail. Since data seems error-free, require two consistent seeds; fallback: single-seed hits get full verification.\n\nHmm, but reads spanning deletion breakpoints: seeds at the left end map upstream of the breakpoint, seeds at the right end map downstream. This is exactly the split-read signal! So we should not just record \"mapped/unmapped\" but collect all seed hits:\n- For each read: collect hits for seed A (offset 0..25), seed B (offset 62..87), seed C (offset 125..150).\n- If A, B, C all consistent (same position) → fully aligned read → use for depth + proper pair stats.\n- If A/B map to one location and C maps elsewhere (or vice versa) → split-read candidate → record split-read evidence with approximate breakpoint.\n- If nothing maps