ations of both mates.\n\nInsert size: for FR pairs: R1 at pos1 on + strand, R2 at pos2 on − strand, pos2 >= pos1, fragment = pos2 + 150 - pos1... let me define: fragment_start = min(pos), fragment_end = max(pos) + read_len. Insert span = fragment_end - fragment_start. Expected insert size for simulated data: unknown — estimate from the data (median of concordant pairs). From mean depth ~2x and 332k pairs × insert ≈ 99.6Mb/50.8Mb… 2x coverage: total bases = 99.6M = 332123 pairs × fragment length → mean fragment ≈ 300bp. So insert ~300bp (i.e., ~300bp fragments of 2×150 — nearly overlapping). Discordant threshold: span > ~800bp (or median + 10*MAD). Deletion-spanning pairs will have span ≈ deletion_size + insert ≈ huge (kb to Mb).\n\nHmm wait: with 300bp fragments and 150bp reads, pairs nearly overlap (300bp fragment). Depth 2x. OK.\n\nFor a large deletion (say 1Mb):\n- Depth inside ≈ 0 (homozygous) or ~1x (heterozygous).\n- Spanning pairs: pairs where one mate maps near the left breakpoint and the other near the right → span ≈ deletion size. In 2x coverage of a 1Mb deletion, boundary-spanning pairs are rare but present (a few dozen).\n- Split reads: reads starting right at the breakpoint: also rare (~coverage × 2 boundaries ≈ 2×2×... each boundary crossed by reads starting within 150bp of the breakpoint: ~2×150×2 = 600 reads... wait coverage 2x → at any given base, ~2 fragments cover it. Reads spanning the exact breakpoint junction: ~2 per boundary × … for a homozygous deletion, all fragments at the junction span it. Expected split reads ≈ coverage × 2 boundaries ≈ 4 split reads? Hmm, coverage 2x: at each base of the reference adjacent to the deletion, ~2 read pairs cover it. A split read must cover the junction: fragments starting within 149bp upstream of the left breakpoint: ~150 × 2 = ~300 fragments per boundary?? No wait — coverage 2x means ~2 fragments covering each base. Fragments starting within a window of length 150 upstream of the breakpoint: ~2 × 150 = ~300? No: fragments per base = coverage/read_length ≈ 2/300 per base. In a 150bp window: ~1 fragment. So ~1 split read per boundary for 2x coverage?? Too few. Hmm, coverage = N_pairs × fragment_length / genome → 332123 × 300 / 50.8M ≈ 1.96x. Fragments starting per base ≈ coverage/fragment_length ≈ 0.0065. In 150bp window ≈ ~1 fragment. So ~1 split read per boundary — very few. But discordant pairs: pairs where either mate falls within ~fragment_length of the boundary... same scale ~1 per boundary?\n\nHmm wait, that's the number of fragments whose start is within 150bp upstream of the breakpoint. But discordant pairs: a pair is discordant-spanning if one mate maps within ~fragment_length upstream of the left breakpoint AND the other maps downstream of the right breakpoint. For large deletions with fixed ~300bp fragment size, spanning pairs ≈ number of fragments covering the left breakpoint region (within ~300bp upstream) ≈ 300 × 0.0065 ≈ ~2 per boundary as well.\n\nHmm, so at 2x coverage, junction-spanning evidence is sparse (~a few reads total). But depth evidence is very strong (a megabase-scale region with zero/half coverage). The task says \"distinguish evidence from precision limits\" — meaning: the rounded 100kb breakpoints are the precision limit of the depth signal; split reads/discordant pairs (if any) give higher-precision breakpoints, and we should report both and state which is which.\n\nOK, my analysis framework is right. Let me write the code. I'll make the alignment step efficient, then compute:\n\n1. Depth profile at 1kb and 100kb.\n2. Candidate deleted region: contiguous 100kb bins with depth < threshold (say < 0.25× genome median).\n3. Discordant pairs: FR pairs with span > 2kb (very safe threshold given 300bp fragments) → collect; cluster midpoints.\n4. Split reads: reads with two alignment clusters.\n5. Refine breakpoints: if split reads/discordant pairs define them, refined_start/end = min/max of junction positions; else use depth drop boundaries (last covered bin before drop, first covered bin after).\n6. Round to 100kb.\n\nOK let me write output/analysis.py. I'll aim for clean, documented, deterministic code. Then run it.\n\nActually one more consideration about index memory: sorted hashes 50.8M uint64 (406MB) + int64 argsort output (406MB) + int32 positions (203MB) + codes (51MB) + temporaries. Peak ~1.3GB. Should be fine on a modern machine. I could reduce by using stride 2 (every other k-mer): halves memory; seeds are 25-mers so stride 2 still hits every seed (the index contains all k-mers starting at even positions; a query seed matches at its own start position if that's even, otherwise the k-mer at pos-1 (which is in the index) shares 24 of 25 bases — no, exact-match lookup requires the exact k-mer to be in the index; a seed at odd start is not indexed). With stride 2, each read seed has a 50% chance of missing → with 3 seeds per strand, P(all miss) = 12.5%... too high. Better to index all k-mers but with k=21 (same count — k doesn't change count, just hash collisions; k=21 hashes still 50 bits). Count stays at ~50.8M-k. Keep all k-mers, k=25.\n\nAlternative to reduce memory: np.argsort with kind='stable' still returns int64. Can use uint32 for positions (chr22 < 2^32 ✓). Sort: np.argsort(H, kind='radix'? 'stable') — fine.\n\nActually, cleaner approach: pack hash+position... no, keep it simple. Let's go.\n\nEdge: hash computation h = h*4 + c, c is in {0..4}; N-containing k-mers (c=4) — to avoid collisions, set their hash to a dedicated large value: after the loop, if a k-mer contained any N → hash = MAX. Track: bad = bad | (C[j:...]==4); h[bad]=UINT64_MAX. Searchsorted queries never match MAX unless the seed itself contains N (seeds from reads: reads appear all ACGT; if a seed contains N → skip that seed).\n\nPer-read alignment loop in Python: 664k iterations with small overhead — optimize: precompute for each seed index s (0..6N-1): lo, hi, multiplicity. Then iterate reads; for each read iterate its 6