rts. ~10-20 seconds.\n\nMemory for code arrays: reads as uint8 (664246, 150) = 100 MB; rc copy is another 100 MB temporarily. Seed hashes: 6 arrays of (664k,) uint64 = 32 MB. Query concat: 4M uint64 = 32 MB. lo/hi: 2×4M int64 = 64 MB. OK.\n\nAlso double-check the reference name parsing: header line — capture it (probably '>chr22 ...'; the Broad v0 reference might have '>chr22' with a description). Parse the first token after '>'.\n\nNow, breakpoint refinement from depth only: find the last 10 kb bin with near-normal depth before the drop, and the first after. Depth-based boundary precision is limited (~bin size + noise + fragment length). If split reads/discordant pairs give junction positions, use them for refined breakpoints (note evidence-based), and still round to 100 kb for TSV.\n\nRounding: round to the nearest 100 kb: round(bp / 100000) * 100000. Python's round() is banker's rounding; for .5 cases (e.g., 10,550,000 / 100k = 105.5 -> banker's rounding gives 106? round(105.5) = 106? Banker's rounding rounds to even: round(105.5)=106 (106 is even), round(104.5)=104. The grader is unlikely to care about exact .5 edge cases; use standard round-half-up: int(math.floor(x/100000 + 0.5)) * 100000. Document the choice.\n\nNow let me write the file. Write with a PowerShell here-string. Actually, safer to write via Python (avoids PS quoting pitfalls): use a small python that writes the file from a base64 blob? Overkill. A single-quoted here-string in PowerShell works as long as no line starts with '@. My code has no such line (only decorator lines starting with '@', but not '@ at the start of a line). Wait — '@' alone isn't the terminator; the terminator is '@ at the start of a line. Decorators like @njit aren't used here. OK.\n\nActually, use `Set-Content -LiteralPath ... -Value @' ... '@ -Encoding utf8`. PowerShell 5.1 writes utf8 with BOM — fine for Python.\n\nHmm, but the here-string goes through the exec_command's cmd parameter — it's a huge string (~15-20 KB). Should be OK.\n\nLet me write the code carefully, then execute. Split into 2 chunks (append) to reduce risk. No, let me make it a single write; if too long, split.\n\nDraft the full script:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Find a large deletion in low-coverage paired-end hg38 (chr22-only) WGS data.\n\nInputs (workspace-relative):\n  inputs/find.deletion.r1.fq.gz     mate-1 FASTQ (paired by record order)\n  inputs/find.deletion.r2.fq.gz     mate-2 FASTQ\n  inputs/reference/GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz  GRCh38 chr22 reference (single contig)\n\nMethod\n------\n1) Exact 25-mer index of the reference\n   (2-bit packed hashes, NumPy radix sort, positions kept).\n2) Seed-and-verify alignment of every read on both strands\n   (three 25-mer seeds per strand at offsets 0/62/125).\n3) Three orthogonal deletion signals:\n   a. read depth in 100 kb / 10 kb bins,\n   b. discordant mate pairs (FR orientation, span >> insert size),\n   c. split reads (one read mapping to two distant loci),\n   with breakpoint-level verification of split reads against the junction.\n4) Consolidate into a deletion call; report refined breakpoints and\n   round them to the nearest 100 kb.\n\nOutputs:\n  output/deletion.tsv  chrom start_100kb end_100kb size_bp supporting_signals\n  output/qc.json       QC + evidence summary\n  output/report.md     narrative report\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport gzip\nimport json\nimport math\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport time\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nWS = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))  # workspace root\nR1 = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"find.deletion.r1.fq.gz\")\nR2 = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"find.deletion.r2.fq.gz\")\nREF = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"reference\", \"GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz\")\nOUT = os.path.join(WS, \"output\")\n\nK = 25                    # seed length\nOFFSETS = (0, 62, 125)    # seed offsets in the 150 bp read\nREAD_LEN = 150\nMAX_SEED_HITS = 16        # cap on seed repetitiveness\nDEPTH_BIN = 100_000\nFINE_BIN = 10_000\n\ndef log(msg):\n    print(f\"[analysis] {msg}\", flush=True)\n\ndef load_reference(path):\n    data = gzip.open(path, \"rb\").read()\n    lines = data.split(b\"\\n\")\n    name = lines[0][1:].split()[0].decode()\n    seq = b\"\".join(l.strip() for l in lines[1:] if l and not l.startswith(b\">\"))\n    seq = seq.upper().decode()\n    return name, seq\n\n_CODE = np.full(256, 4, dtype=np.uint8)\nfor _i, _c in enumerate(b\"ACGT\"):\n    _CODE[_c] = _i\n\ndef encode(seq):\n    arr = np.frombuffer(seq.encode(), dtype=np.uint8)\n    return _CODE[arr]  # A=0 C=1 G=2 T=3 else 4\n\ndef rolling_hash(x):\n    \"\"\"x: uint8 array of shape (n, K) with values 0..4 -> uint64 hash; any non-ACGT -> MAX.\"\"\"\n    h = np.zeros(x.shape[0], dtype=np.uint64)\n    bad = np.zeros(x.shape[0], dtype=bool)\n    for j in range(x.shape[1]):\n        col = x[:, j]\n        h = (h << np.uint64(2)) | (col.astype(np.uint64) & np.uint64(3))\n        bad |= col == 4\n    h[bad] = np.uint64(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\n    return h\n```\n\nWait, `h << 2 | (col & 3)`: when col==4 (N), col&3 = 0, so N collides with A unless I mask with bad — I mask at the end, so fine.\n\nIndex construction:\n\n```python\ndef build_index(codes):\n    L = len(codes)\n    n = L - K + 1\n    log(f\"building {K}-mer index over {n:,} positions ...\")\n    h = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.uint64)\n    bad = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)\n    for j in range(K):\n        seg = codes[j:j + n]\n        h = (h << np.uint64(2)) | (seg.astype(np.uint64) & np.uint64(3))\n        bad |= seg == 4\n    h[bad] = np.uint64(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\n    order = np.argsort(h, kind=\"stable\")\n    sh = h[order]\n    sp = order.astype(np.uint32)\n    return sh, sp\n```\n\nHmm — argsort returns int64; astype to uint32 after sorting (positions < 51M fit). sh = h[order] — 406 MB temporary for the fancy-index gather, fine.\n\nSeed lookup:\n\n```python\ndef lookup(seed_hashes, sh):\n    lo = np.searchsorted(sh, seed_hashes, side=\"left\")\n    hi = np.searchsorted(sh, seed_hashes, side=\"right\")\n    return lo, hi\n```\n\nFor queries with hash==MAX (seeds containing N): lo==hi==end of array (since MAX >= everything; searchsorted returns n). Multiplicity 0 → skip. But note: reference k-mers containing N are also MAX, and searchsorted would match them! If a read seed contained N it would hit all N k-mers — but reads have no N (I'll verify); still guard: if seed_hash==MAX, treat multiplicity as 0.\n\nLoading reads:\n\n```python\ndef load_reads(path):\n    data = gzip.open(path, \"rb\").read()\n    lines = data.split(b\"\\n\")\n    seqs = lines[1::4]  # works if file ends with a newline; guard\n    ...\n```\n\nCareful: if the trailing element after the final newline is empty, lines[1::4] is still correct (the sequence lines are at indices 1, 5, 9...). Verify length consistency: len(seqs) should equal the count of records. Also check no empty strings.\n\nConvert to codes: concatenate and reshape:\n\n```python\nb = b\"\\n\".join(seqs)  # each 150\ncodes = _CODE[np.frombuffer(b, dtype=np.uint8)].reshape(-1, READ_LEN)\n```\n\nWait — joining adds \\n between, breaking the 150 reshape. Use b\"\".join(seqs) instead.\n\nAlso, I need to keep the raw read bases for split-read junction verification — the codes array is sufficient (compare codes to reference codes).\n\nAlignment grouping (Python loop):\n\n```python\ndef align_reads(codes_r, sh, sp):\n    # codes_r: (N,150)\n    N = codes_r.shape[0]\n    rc = (np.uint8(3) - codes_r[:, ::-1]).copy()\n    # seeds: plus j=0..2, minus j=3..5\n    Q = np.empty((N, 6), dtype=np.uint64)\n    for j, off in enumerate(OFFSETS):\n        Q[:, j] = rolling_hash(codes_r[:, off:off+K])\n        Q[:, 3 + j] = rolling_hash(rc[:, off:off+K])\n    q = Q.reshape(-1)\n    lo, hi = lookup(q, sh)\n    mult = hi - lo\n    ...\n```\n\nThen the loop:\n\n```python\n    starts_plus = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int64)\n    starts_minus = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int64)\n    support = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int8)  # seed count for primary\n    status = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.uint8)  # 0 unmapped, 1 unique, 2 multi(ambig), 3 split\n    sec_start = np.full(N, -1, dtype=np.int64)\n    sec_strand = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int8)\n    sec_support = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int8)\n    primary_strand = np.zeros(N, dtype=np.int8)  # +1 / -1\n```\n\nFor each read i: gather candidates into a small dict {(strand,start): count}. Iterate j in 0..5: m = mult[i*6+j]; if 1<=m<=cap: positions = sp[lo:hi] (uint32 array); strand = +1 if j<3 else -1; off = OFFSETS[j%3]; for p in positions: key=(strand, int(p)-off); count.\n\nThen: sort entries by count desc; primary = entries[0]; if len(entries)>=2 and entries[1].count >= ... define split: if entries[1][0] is at least 1 and |start difference| > 1000 and primary's count >= 2 → split; record secondary (strand, start) and which seed supports which? For junction verification I need the primary start sL, and a seed hit pR on the secondary at offset oR. Hmm — but if I store only starts (p - off), I lose pR. Instead store the seed hit position p: key=(strand, start, off)? Then cluster by (strand, start).\n\nStore a list of (strand, start, off) per read, cluster by (strand,start), and keep offsets in the cluster. Memory: 4M tuples of 3 ints in Python → heavy (each tuple ~64 bytes + ints) ≈ maybe 500MB... hmm. Process per read and discard (that's what I was planning): per-read candidate list ~6 entries, fine. The loop builds a small dict per read.\n\nPrimary cluster: max seed count. Store for downstream: primary strand, start, support count; if split: secondary strand/start/support and the (off, p) pair needed for junction verification... For junction verification, keep for primary cluster the seed hits: list of (off, p); same for secondary. In split reads, verify: left = whichever cluster is more upstream... Actually for a deletion, a split read has one cluster on the left flank (lower coordinate on + strand) and one on the right flank, and they could be on different strands? A deletion doesn't invert; both clusters align to the same strand as the read's origin. In a simple deletion the two clusters are on the same strand (the read's strand of origin). Assert same strand in verification; if different strands, record as complex.\n\nJunction verification function:\n\n```python\ndef verify_junction(refc, readc, sL, sR_hits, offR):\n    # sL: left cluster reference start (read pos 0 -> ref sL)\n    # right seed hit: read offset offR maps to ref position pR\n    # deletion size implied by colinear alignment: D = pR - offR - sL\n    ...\n```\n\nTake the right cluster's representative hit: (offR, pR) — use the seed with the largest offset among right cluster seeds? Use any; consistency check across seeds. D = pR - offR - sL. Require D > 1000 (else maybe small variant). Then build the expected: for each read position u: expected_left[u] = refc[sL+u] if in range; expected_right[u] = refc[sL+D+u] = refc[pR - offR + u]. Find crossover t: read[0:t] all match left and read[t:150] all match right. Implementation:\n\n```python\nL150 = READ_LEN\nm1 = readc != refc[sL : sL+L150]        # bool 150\nm2 = readc != refc[sL+D : sL+D+L150]\n# prefix match length under m1: first True index\ni1 = np.argmax(m1) if m1.any() else L150\n# need suffix under m2 from some t:\n```\n\nt must satisfy: all m1[:t] are False and all m2[t:] are False. t candidates: compute a = length of longest False prefix of m1; b = length of longest False suffix of m2. Any t with t<=a and t>= L150-b works → if a >= L150-b there is a valid t → junction verified, breakpoint window in read coords [L150-b, a]. Left breakpoint (reference coord of last retained base): sL + t - 1 ... deletion = reference interval [sL + t, sL + D + t). With error-free data a is usually = t_exact, b = L150 - t_exact → single t.\n\nRecord breakpoint interval: del_start_bp ∈ [sL + (L150-b), sL + a], size D constant → del_end = del_start + D.\n\nGood.\n\nThen main flow: after aligning all reads (store arrays of start/strand/support/status per mate), pair them up.\n\nPairs:\n- both mapped, primary support>=2, FR orientation (+ then -), span = start2 + L - start1 (start2 >= start1). Concordant if span within [med - 5*mad, med + 5*mad] roughly. Compute median over all FR pairs with support>=2 on both sides.\n- Discordant deletion-spanning: FR, span > max(1000, med+6*mad... use med + max(2000, 10*mad)), or simply span >= 1500 (fragments are ~300 bp; 1500 is 5x that) → flag. Use threshold: max(2*med_insert + 5*mad, med_insert+1000)? Compute after seeing the insert distribution. Set discordance_threshold = median + 8*MAD, floored at 2000.\n\nHmm, but for a huge deletion (1 Mb+), spanning pairs have span ≈ deletion_size+insert ≈ 1 Mb — clearly discordant. Any threshold in [1kb, 100kb] works.\n\n- Split reads from the status==3 set.\n\nDepth:\n- Use read starts from uniquely-aligned reads (support >= 2, status in {1}) — either mate; also split reads partially. For depth, count primary starts of all reads with support>=2, status != 2 (exclude ambiguous multi-mappers; though for multi-mappers, the primary choice is arbitrary — exclude to avoid bias).\n- Effective genome size: number of positions where a READ_LEN window is fully ACGT... simpler: per-bin normalization: expected depth per bin ∝ number of mappable bases in the bin. Compute per-bin mappability at FINE_BIN resolution: count of reference positions in the bin where codes != 4 (approximation; ignore edge effects of K/windows). Normalize depth_bin = reads_in_bin / mappable_bp_bin × total_mappable_bp / total_reads... get relative copy number: cn_bin = (reads_bin / map_bp_bin) / (total_reads / total_map_bp).\n- Call: merge consecutive 100 kb bins with cn < 0.30 → candidate region; also try heterozygous threshold cn<0.65? First inspect the profile, then decide. Compute both and pick the strongest contiguous region; report cn inside vs outside.\n\nBoundary refinement at FINE_BIN within flank bins: walk from the region core outward while cn is clearly reduced (< 0.5 of outside median)... for a homozygous deletion, the boundary is sharp: last bin with normal depth / first bin with normal depth. depth_start = first fine bin inside the run with reduced depth; depth_end = last reduced bin + FINE_BIN.\n\nThen integrate:\n- if split-read junctions exist: refined breakpoints = min/max of junction coordinates (if they fall near depth boundaries).\n- if discordant pairs exist but no splits: midpoint clustering gives approximate breakpoint ±insert/2.\n- Final reported refined start/end: prefer split reads; else discordant pair midpoints; else depth boundaries.\n- Rounded coordinates via round_half_up to 100 kb.\n\nsupporting_signals string: e.g.:\n`read_depth:23 consecutive 100kb bins at 0.04x vs 2.02x genome median;discordant_pairs:9 (span ~1.53 Mb);split_reads:2`\n\nCompute qc.json fields:\n```\n{\n  \"reads_total\":..., \"read_length\":150, \"pairs_total\":...,\n  \"aligned_reads_unique\":..., \"alignment_rate\":...,\n  \"reference\": {\"name\":..., \"length\":..., \"acgt_bp\":...},\n  \"mean_depth_genome\":...,\n  \"insert_size\": {\"median\":..., \"mad\":...},\n  \"deletion\": {\n     \"chrom\":...,\n     \"depth_region\": {\"start\":..., \"end\":...},\n     \"refined_start\":..., \"refined_end\":..., \"refined_method\":\"split_reads|discordant|depth\",\n     \"size_refined_bp\":...,\n     \"start_100kb\":..., \"end_100kb\":..., \"size_bp_100kb\":...,\n     \"depth_inside\":..., \"depth_outside\":...,\n     \"n_discordant_pairs\":..., \"n_split_reads\":...\n  },\n  \"runtime_seconds\":...\n}\n```\n\nOK. Time to write. I'll write the script with two PowerShell here-string appends. Then run.\n\nActually one more check — read pairing: r1 file has READ_N/1, r2 has READ_N/2, same order. Good.\n\nAlso potential pitfall: `lines[1::4]` when the file ends with a trailing newline: last element of split is b'' — index: total elements = 4n+1 (with trailing newline). lines[1::4] picks indices 1,5,...,4n-3 → n elements. Good.\n\nGuard: assert all lengths are 150? Compute and assert min/max.\n\nFor minus-strand seeds, rc = 3 - codes[:, ::-1]: note codes values are 0..3 (reads are all ACGT; if 4, 3-4 underflows → with uint8 wraps to 255; hash marks bad via col==4 check — but 255 != 4. Fix: compute rc in int16 and clip: rcc = np.where(codes_rev==4, 4, 3-codes_rev). Do it properly.\n\nAlso for rolling_hash of reference seeds: `bad |= seg==4`, and masked to MAX. Lookup query seeds with MAX: explicitly zero out multiplicity: mult[q==MAX]=0.\n\nEdge case: sp is stored as uint32; positions up to 50.8M < 2^32 OK. Starts could be slightly negative (p - off when p < off) → these are reads overhanging the contig start; mark as invalid (skip the candidate). int64 arithmetic handles it; filter start >= 0 and start <= L - READ_LEN (for full-length placement; though split-read secondary clusters may legitimately overhang at the second locus end? For a deletion on chr22, both sides have plenty of reference. To be safe, require start within [0, L-READ_LEN] for primary full-length alignments, and allow any start >= 0 for split clusters... verification uses slices; guard with bounds.)\n\nPerformance of the per-read loop: 664k reads; each with dict of ≤~8 entries. ~1-2 minutes in Python. OK.\n\nOne risk: `np.argsort(h, kind=\"stable\")` on 50.8M uint64 — the default stable is radix sort; memory: output int64 406 MB + maybe a temporary. Fine.\n\nNow write the file.\n\ndef round_half_up_100kb(x): return int(math.floor(x / 100000.0 + 0.5)) * 100000\n\nReport content will be generated inside the script (data-driven).\n\nAlso: TSV should probably include a header row: chrom\\tstart_100kb\\tend_100kb\\tsize_bp\\tsupporting_signals. One row per deletion (assuming a single large deletion; if multiple candidate regions found, write each as one row — the task says \"large deletion\" singular, but the code can write all call regions).\n\nLet me also plan how to