 b = READ_LEN - argmax(m2[::-1]) → position of last match... no wait, argmax(m2[::-1]) finds the first True (mismatch) from the end; suffix [b, L) all matches. If last base mismatches, b = READ_LEN (empty suffix). Then a=150 ≥ b=150 → passes with the crossover t=150, meaning \"the entire read matches the left locus and the right side contributes nothing\" — a spurious split read!\n\nThat's the bug: I'm allowing trivial crossovers where one side contributes zero bases. A genuine split read needs both sides to have substantial support (say ≥20bp each). Fix: require t ∈ [min_side, L - min_side], i.e., a ≥ L - ... let me redefine: valid crossover t must satisfy b ≤ t ≤ a and min_side ≤ t ≤ L - min_side with min_side ~ 30. Also, should we only accept split candidates where the secondary seed is actually consistent with the crossover (i.e., the secondary seed offset off2 is ≥ t)? The seed at offset off2 matches the right locus, so t ≤ off2 + K... actually, the seed covers [off2, off2+25) of the read and aligns to the right locus, so for consistency the crossover must be t ≤ off2 (the seed lies entirely on the right side). Similarly, the left-side seeds (those supporting the primary) require t ≥ offL + 25. My a/b computation captures most of this implicitly, but explicit constraints would be cleaner.\n\nAnother issue: 447 discordant pairs also feels like too many for spanning pairs at 2.6x coverage. Expected spanning pairs ≈ coverage × 2 boundaries × P(fragment spans)... For a 1Mb deletion with ~2.6x depth (fragments ~500bp), the number of fragments crossing each boundary ≈ depth × fragment_length / fragment_length... hmm: fragments per bp = depth/fragment_length = 2.6/500 ≈ 0.0052. Fragments starting within 500bp upstream of the breakpoint and extending past it: ≈ 500 × 0.0052 ≈ 2.6 per boundary → ~5 total. 447 is way more — probably includes false discordants (e.g., pairs with one mate mismapped due to repeats, or both mates hitting a repetitive region). Not a problem — 