{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"reasoning","text":"As expected: the split-read validation is producing thousands of false clusters (mostly tiny \"deletions\" of 150–1000 bp — these are reads containing small indels/errors where my colinear two-locus model is faking a junction), and the \"best\" cluster (n=8 at chr22:42.5M) has nothing to do with the depth call (20.0–21.0 Mb). The refined breakpoints ended up following the bogus cluster, corrupting the reported coordinates (42.5M instead of 20M).\n\nProblems to fix:\n1. **Split-read validation must require a minimum flank length on both sides** (e.g., ≥30 bp), and the implied deletion size D must be ≥ some minimum (e.g., ≥1 kb, or at least > READ_LEN). Small \"deletions\" of 150–1000 bp are either small variants or alignment artifacts, not the target large deletion.\n2. **Split clusters must be consistent with the depth region**: breakpoints should flank the depth region (left breakpoint near the depth start, right = left + D near the depth end). Better yet: filter split candidates to those with breakpoints within ±10 kb of the depth boundaries and with D within ±20% of the depth region size. This is legitimate — depth defines the region, and split reads refine the breakpoints.\n3. **Discordant pairs**: filter similarly — one mate near the left boundary, the other near the right boundary, and span ≈ depth region size.\n4. Additionally, require the crossover to be consistent with the secondary seed offset: t ≤ off2.\n\nAlso, the huge qc.json containing 6,912 clusters is bloated (270k tokens). Cap the number of clusters reported in JSON (e.g., report only the top 10 clusters after filtering, plus summary counts).\n\nAlso, in the current TSV, size_bp=100000 and start=42500000 — wrong. After fixes: start_100kb should be 20,000,000, end should be 21,000,000, size 1,000,000.\n\nAlso, reconsider the low seed-hit rate (52%). Probably many reads contain simulated sequencing errors. 96.9% of reads map with ≥2 seeds, so no problem.\n\nAnother important improvement: split-read detection should also try seeds at finer offsets near boundaries to refine the breakpoints — but base-level junction validation already gives exact breakpoints from existing seeds: for reads spanning the junction, crossover t is found by comparing m1/m2 — single-base resolution. Good.\n\nBut wait: split candidates are only flagged if the secondary cluster has support ≥1 AND the primary has support ≥2 (or in the special AMBIG case, 1-vs-1). With ~2.6% error per base… hmm wait, is that realistic? Let me actually quantify the error rate: 52% of 25-mer seeds hit → if errors are uniform: (1-e)^25 = 0.52 → e = 0.026. About 4 errors per 150 bp read. For a true split read spanning the junction: seeds: offset 0 (left), 62 (left or right), 125 (right). Each seed hits with probability ~0.52 (no errors within the seed). Primary cluster (left) support ≥2 needs seeds 0 and 62 both to hit: ~27%. And secondary needs seed 125: ~52% → ~14% of true junction reads get flagged as split. Junction coverage ~2.6x → fragments covering the junction per boundary: ~2.6 per boundary… hmm wait, that's the number of fragments covering each base… Let me compute: fragments per bp = depth/fragment length = 2.6/500 = 0.0052. Number of fragments spanning the junction = number of fragments starting in the window (junction-499, junction-1)… a fragment spans the junction if its start is in (junction-499, junction-150]… ≈ 350 × 0.0052 ≈ 1.8 fragments per boundary. So ~2 split reads per boundary before the error filter, ~0.3 after. Hmm — the true junction split reads may be very few (0–4). Discordant pairs similarly ~2 per boundary.\n\nSo junction-level evidence is sparse. Depth evidence (10 bins of 100kb with CN ~0.002) is the dominant evidence, and breakpoint precision is fundamentally limited — this is exactly the \"distinguish evidence from precision limits\" part. In the report: depth defines [20.00M, 21.00M] on fine bins — wait, refine_boundaries returned exactly 20,000,000-21,000,000 at 10kb resolution. The simulation probably placed breakpoints on a 100kb grid. If discordant/split reads confirm, great; otherwise depth-only precision ~10 kb.\n\nLet me verify whether the 447 discordant pairs contain any spanning pairs: the median span of the 447 is 868,451 bp — oh, that's very close to… wait, depth region is 20-21Mb (1Mb deletion). Spanning pair span ≈ deletion size + insert ≈ 1,000,500. Median 868,451 — hmm, doesn't match 1Mb. And with span ≥ 2000 threshold, 447 pairs: probably mostly mismapped pairs (repeats). Let me look at the distribution of discordant pair spans and their relationship to the depth region. I'll add a filter requiring mate positions to flank the depth region.\n\nActually wait — the depth region might be wider than 1Mb? The low-depth run is exactly bins 200-209 (20.0-21.0 Mb). CN inside is 0.002 — essentially zero → homozygous. OK.\n\nNow, why is the median discordant span 868k? If some pairs really span the 1Mb deletion, span ≈ 1,000,500 ± insert noise. 868k ≠ 1000.5k. Hmm. Maybe many discordant pairs are from a different artifact: mismapping to paralogous segments (chr22 has large segmental duplications, e.g., LCR22s near 14-17Mb and pericentromeric regions). Pairs with one mate mismapped can show any span.\n\nLet me look at the actual data: for the 447 discordant pairs, where are their mates relative to the depth region [20M, 21M)? Add diagnostics: for each discordant pair compute (start1, start2, span), count those with start1 < 20M < 21M < start2+150. Also histogram of spans.\n\nPlan: patch analysis.py:\n1. verify_split_junction: MIN_SIDE=30; require valid crossover t in [max(b, MIN_SIDE), min(a, READ_LEN-MIN_SIDE)]; also require D >= 1000. Also require t ≤ off2 (secondary seed on the right side) — pass off2. And require the secondary seed to be on the right side of the crossover: t <= off2.\n2. In main(): after depth call, filter split reads: keep only clusters with left breakpoint within ±20kb of depth_start and D within [0.5×depth_size, 2×depth_size]… Better yet: require the junction's deleted interval [blo+1, blo+D] to approximately match the depth region: |blo - depth_start| ≤ 50kb and |(blo+D) - depth_end| ≤ 50kb. Then take the best cluster. If none pass the consistency filter, report split reads as supporting=0 (keep raw counts in qc but distinguish them).\n3. Discordant pairs: filter to those flanking the depth region: start1 in [depth_start-5000, depth_start+1000] hmm, actually mate1 (forward, left) should end before depth_start: start1 + READ_LEN ≤ depth_start + 500… Let me do: start1 < depth_start + 1000 and start2 + READ_LEN > depth_end - 1000 and start2 ≥ depth_end - 1500 - … Simplest robust version: start1 ≤ depth_start + 2000 and start2 ≥ depth_end - 2000 - READ_LEN and span ≥ T. Count them; get median span; estimate D = span - insert_median; breakpoint window from those pairs: left breakpoint ∈ [start1 + READ_LEN - ... ] hmm; for a spanning pair with fragment length F: fragment = read1(150) + gap + read2(150); F = span… span = start2 + 150 - start1 = D + F_true. Left breakpoint ≈ start1 + F_true - (something)… Fragment's alignment: the reference interval [start1, start2+150) minus the deleted interval [B1, B2) has length F_true ≈ insert. So B1 - start1 + (start2+150 - B2) = F_true, and B2 - B1 = D. Two unknowns, one equation → can't pinpoint both breakpoints from a single pair; the window for B1 is [start1+150, start2+150-F_true]… B1 ≥ start1+150 (read1 fully on the left) and B2 ≤ start2 (read2 fully on the right) → B1 ∈ [start1+150, start2 - D]… wait B2 = B1 + D ≤ start2 → B1 ≤ start2 - D. Also B1 ≥ start1 + 150. So B1 ∈ [start1+150, start2-D]. Width = span - D - 150 = F_true - 150 ≈ 350. OK — report the intersection/union window across pairs.\n\n   For the breakpoint estimate: take B1_lo = max of (start1+150) across pairs? And B1_hi = min of (start2 - D)? If pairs are consistent, the window narrows. Simpler: report per-pair windows and take the median of [B1_lo, B1_hi] midpoints.\n\n   Actually even simpler: if we have filtered spanning pairs, D_est = span - insert_median; breakpoint uncertainty is ~fragment length. Use midpoints for refined_start/end, with a note on precision.\n\n4. Refine integration priority: if split-read clusters are consistent → single-base breakpoints; else if there are consistent discordant pairs → ±fragment; else depth boundaries (±10 kb).\n5. qc.json: report only the top 10 split clusters (by support) after filtering, plus counts. Also add discordant pair span stats, a small sample of spanning pairs, and a depth profile (CN per big bin) across the whole chromosome? That might be useful for the report — ~508 bins; fine as a compact list of rounded values… actually 508 floats in JSON is fine.\n6. Also fix the supporting_signals string in the TSV.\n\nAlso double-check the depth region boundaries at 10kb: refine returned exactly 20,000,000-21,000,000. But let me add a diagnostic dump of fine-bin CN around the boundaries to qc so we can eyeball sharpness.\n\nLet me implement the patches. Changes:\n\nA) verify_split_junction signature: add min_side=30, require D >= 1000 and t ≤ off2:\n\n```python\ndef verify_split_junction(refc, readc, s1, s2, off2, p2, min_side=30, min_deletion=1000):\n    D = p2 - off2 - s1\n    if D < min_deletion:\n        return False, -1, -1, D\n    need = s1 + D + READ_LEN\n    if need > len(refc):\n        return False, -1, -1, D\n    m1 = readc != refc[s1:s1 + READ_LEN]\n    m2 = readc != refc[s1 + D:s1 + D + READ_LEN]\n    a = int(np.argmax(m1)) if m1.any() else READ_LEN\n    if not m2.any():\n        b = READ_LEN\n    else:\n        b = READ_LEN - int(np.argmax(m2[::-1]))\n    t_lo = max(b, min_side)\n    t_hi = min(a, READ_LEN - min_side, off2)\n    if t_hi >= t_lo:\n        return True, s1 + t_lo, s1 + t_hi + 1, D\n    return False, -1, -1, D\n```\n\nHmm wait, t ≤ off2: the crossover must be at or before the secondary seed start (so the secondary seed is entirely on the right side). Yes.\n\nBut note: what if the read's left side also matches somewhere with a shift (repeats)? The min_side and consistency check is enough for our purpose.\n\nB) Split collection in main(): after computing all validated splits, filter: keep those whose breakpoint is within ±50kb of the depth boundaries and whose D is within 50% of the depth size. Cluster those. Keep raw counts too.\n\nLet me restructure main() accordingly:\n\n```python\n    # ---------------- split reads ----------------\n    splits_raw = []\n    for arr, cd in ((a1, c1), (a2, c2)):\n        idx = np.nonzero(arr[\"status\"] == SPLIT)[0]\n        for i in idx:\n            ok, blo, bhi, D = verify_split_junction(...)\n            if ok:\n                splits_raw.append((blo, bhi, D))\n    depth_size = depth_end - depth_start\n    def consistent(blo, D):\n        return (abs((blo + 1) - depth_start) <= 50_000 and\n                abs(D - depth_size) <= max(50_000, int(0.2 * depth_size)))\n    splits = [s for s in splits_raw if consistent(s[0], s[2])]\n    ... clustering on splits ...\n```\n\nHmm wait: depth_start (0-based) = 19,999,999 → 1-based start = depth_start+1 = 20,000,000. blo is the 0-based left breakpoint (first deleted base = blo+1). Consistency: |(blo+1) - (depth_start+1)| = |blo - depth_start| ≤ 50kb. And |(blo + D) - depth_end| ≤ … right end 1-based = blo + D; depth_end (exclusive 0-based) = last deleted 1-based. So compare blo+D vs depth_end. Good.\n\nC) Filter discordant pairs to those flanking the depth region:\n\n```python\n    d_start0, d_end0 = depth_start, depth_end  # 0-based, end exclusive\n    flank_ok = (a1[\"start\"][disc_idx] <= d_start0 + 2000) & \\\n               (a2[\"start\"][disc_idx] >= d_end0 - 2000 - READ_LEN)\n    disc_idx2 = disc_idx[flank_ok]\n```\n\nWait: mate2 starts after the right boundary: start2 ≥ d_end0 - 2000 or so. Mate2 is on the minus strand, covering [start2, start2+150). For it to be entirely right of the deletion: start2 ≥ B2 ≥ d_end0 - slack. Use start2 ≥ d_end0 - 2000. Mate1 forward [start1, start1+150) entirely to the left: start1 + READ_LEN ≤ B1 ≤ d_start0 + slack → start1 ≤ d_start0 + 2000. Let me do:\n\n```python\n    flank_ok = (a1[\"start\"][disc_idx] + READ_LEN >= d_start0 - 5000) & \\\n               (a1[\"start\"][disc_idx] <= d_start0 + 2000) & \\\n               (a2[\"start\"][disc_idx] >= d_end0 - 2000) & \\\n               (a2[\"start\"][disc_idx] <= d_end0 + 5000)\n```\n\nThen compute span for those, D_est = span - insert median. Breakpoint window per pair: B1 ∈ [start1+READ_LEN, start2 - D_est]… wait B2 ≤ start2 → B1 ≤ start2 - D_est. Hmm B2 = B1 + D ≤ start2 → B1 ≤ start2 - D. And B1 ≥ start1 + READ_LEN (read1 fully on the left). If window is empty (start2 - D < start1+150) skip the pair (inconsistent). Collect windows; refined_start from median of window midpoints… Actually to keep it simple and honest:\n\n- Refined start window: [max(start1+READ_LEN) across pairs, min(start2 - D_est)]\n- If lo <= hi: refined_start = (lo+hi)//2, precision = hi-lo.\n\nHmm, but with 0–2 pairs expected… let me see what the data gives. The previous run found 447 discordant pairs with median span 868k — after flanking filter we'll see how many remain.\n\nD) qc.json: report only top 10 clusters after filtering (by support); raw split counts before filter; discordant counts before/after filter; per-pair spanning list (cap at 50); depth profile: big-bin CN array rounded to 3 decimals (508 values) — include as \"depth_cn_profile_100kb\". Fine-bin CN around boundaries (±200kb) too for sharpness check.\n\nE) Report: update text accordingly (evidence table, precision discussion).\n\nAlso add: if no consistent split/discordant evidence, refined = depth boundaries, method = 'read_depth', precision ~10kb, and the TSV is unchanged.\n\nAlso fix: supporting_signals should reflect filtered evidence.\n\nLet me rewrite the affected sections of analysis.py. Given the number of changes, rewrite the file wholesale (regenerate analysis.py with all fixes) — that's cleaner than many patches. Rewrite the full script in three here-string chunks again (now with the correct logic), recompile, rerun.\n\nLet me write the final version of the script. Key changes from v1:\n- verify_split_junction with min_side/min_deletion/t≤off2.\n- Consistency filter for splits and discordant pairs against depth region.\n- Bounded qc output.\n- Depth profile in qc.\n- Report tweaks.\n\nAlso fix: the previous \"best cluster\" logic picked the largest n among all clusters — now restricted to consistent ones.\n\nAlso, we might be able to squeeze more junction evidence: split-read detection with finer seeds… the current detection needs the secondary seed to hit exactly; with ~2.6% error rate per base, that's rare. To capture more junction reads, we can also rescue AMBIG reads: reads where all 6 seeds hit different places… already handled by the patch1 fall-through (two clusters, same strand, distant → split candidate). Also reads where exactly one seed hits nowhere… can't do more without a full aligner. Accept sparse junction evidence; depth dominates.\n\nWait — actually let me reconsider the seed-hit rate issue. Only 52% of seeds hit. If the error rate is truly ~2.6%/bp, then P(read maps with ≥2 of 3 seeds per strand) = P(≥2 of 3 seeds error-free) ≈ C(3,2)p²(1-p)+p³, p=0.52 → 3×0.27×0.48 + 0.14 ≈ 0.53 per strand; either strand: ~0.78… but observed alignment rate is 96.9%. So the errors aren't uniform — probably most reads are error-free, and a subset has many errors. Actually the simulation may have variant/SNP blocks… Whatever — alignment is fine.\n\nHmm, one more thing to double-check: the earlier \"300 reads all matched exactly\" test — so most reads are error-free. Then why do 48% of seeds miss? Oh wait — I may have misread: the seeds that missed might be because the seed hashes contain positions filtered by boundary (`if s < 0 or s > L_ref - READ_LEN: continue`) — no, that's after the lookup. Hmm, or MAX_SEED_HITS cap zeroed out 17%… Let me quantify after rerun: count of seeds with mult>16 vs mult==0. Add logging: seeds with 0 hits, seeds with 1 hit, seeds with 2–16 hits, seeds with >16 hits (masked). This will help QC understanding.\n\nActually — another candidate explanation for seed misses: reads whose seed spans the DELETED region junction (won't hit) — but that's only a tiny fraction. Or reads from a contaminant? Let me check unmapped rate per read: 3.1% of reads have <2 seeds — ~20k reads. Hmm, might also be reads with many errors (simulated variants?). Let me add diagnostic logging.\n\nOne more important consideration: if the sample has a heterozygous variant, reads covering the region might contain alternate alleles (not a reference deletion) — but the depth drop to 0.002 strongly suggests a homozygous deletion of 1Mb.\n\nAlso worth checking: could the true deletion be larger, flanked by N gaps? Bins 190-199 (19-20Mb) and 210-219 (21-22Mb): CN outside is 1.028 (median across all outside bins) — no problem.\n\nOK, let me also think about which coordinates to put in the TSV. Breakpoints refined by depth: start 20,000,000 (1-based), end 21,000,000 (1-based inclusive). Round to nearest 100kb: 20,000,000 and 21,000,000. Size = 1,000,000. If split/discordant reads refine to, say, 20,000,001-21,000,000 — same rounded values. Good.\n\nLet me rewrite analysis.py wholesale.\n\nAlso: the task says \"round breakpoints to the nearest 100 kb\" — breakpoints as genomic coordinates. If refined breakpoints are 20,000,000/21,000,000 exactly, rounding is identity.\n\nOK let me write the final script. This time I'll structure main() more carefully. Write v2 in full."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"agent_message","text":"I found that the split-read verifier is producing false positives and pulling refined breakpoints to the wrong location, so I'll rewrite it with proper constraints including minimum flank length, deletion size thresholds, and consistency checks against depth regions.\n\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\chenyuqiong\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$part1 = @'\"'\n#\"'!/usr/bin/env python3\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Find a large deletion in shallow paired-end hg38 (chr22-only) WGS data.\n\nInputs (workspace-relative):\n  inputs/find.deletion.r1.fq.gz        mate-1 FASTQ (paired by record order)\n  inputs/find.deletion.r2.fq.gz        mate-2 FASTQ\n  inputs/reference/GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz  GRCh38 chr22 reference, single contig\n\nMethod (pure Python + NumPy; no external aligner needed):\n\n  1. Build an exact 25-mer index of the reference: 2-bit-packed hashes of\n     every reference 25-mer, radix-sorted with NumPy, positions retained.\n  2. Seed-and-verify alignment: for every read, three seeds per strand at\n     offsets 0/62/125. Seed hits are grouped into candidate alignments and\n     each read is classified as uniquely mapped, ambiguously (multi-) mapped,\n     split (chimeric: two distant same-strand loci), or unmapped.\n  3. Three orthogonal deletion signals:\n       a. read depth in 100 kb (reporting) and 10 kb (refinement) bins,\n          normalized by per-bin mappable (ACGT) reference content;\n       b. discordant mate pairs: FR pairs whose mapped span greatly exceeds\n          the insert-size distribution AND whose mates flank the low-depth\n          region -> they bridge the deletion;\n       c. split reads, verified base-by-base against the implied junction\n          (>= 30 bp on each side, crossover consistent with the seeding),\n          giving single-base breakpoint resolution. Split-read junctions are\n          only accepted as evidence when they are consistent with the\n          depth-defined interval (breakpoint within 50 kb of a depth edge\n          and implied size within 20% of the depth interval size).\n  4. Combine evidence: depth defines the deleted interval (primary evidence\n     for position and zygosity); spanning pairs / split reads refine the\n     breakpoints where available. Coordinates are finally rounded to the\n     nearest 100 kb for deletion.tsv (task-mandated reporting precision),\n     while qc.json and report.md keep the refined coordinates plus the\n     per-signal evidence counts, so evidence can be distinguished from\n     precision limits.\n\nOutputs (workspace-relative):\n  output/deletion.tsv  chrom start_100kb end_100kb size_bp supporting_signals\n  output/qc.json       QC metrics + per-signal evidence\n  output/report.md     narrative report\n\nDeterministic; only touches files inside the workspace.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport gzip\nimport json\nimport math\nimport os\nimport time\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nWS = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))  # workspace root\nR1_PATH = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"find.deletion.r1.fq.gz\")\nR2_PATH = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"find.deletion.r2.fq.gz\")\nREF_PATH = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"reference\", \"GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz\")\nOUT_DIR = os.path.join(WS, \"output\")\n\nK = 25                     # seed length (bp)\nOFFSETS = (0, 62, 125)     # seed offsets inside each 150 bp read\nREAD_LEN = 150\nMAX_SEED_HITS = 16         # seeds hitting more loci are treated as repeats\nBIG_BIN = 100_000          # reporting bin = requested rounding unit\nFINE_BIN = 10_000          # refinement bin for depth boundaries\nMIN_SPLIT_SIDE = 30        # min aligned bp on each side of a junction\nMIN_SPLIT_DEL = 1_000      # split-read implied deletion must be >= this\nEDGE_TOL = 50_000          # split junction may deviate this much from depth edge\n\n# status codes\nUNMAPPED, UNIQUE, AMBIG, SPLIT = 0, 1, 2, 3\n\n\ndef log(msg: str) -> None:\n    print(f\"[analysis] {msg}\", flush=True)\n\n\ndef round_half_up_100kb(x: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Round a coordinate to the nearest 100 kb (half rounds up).\"\"\"\n    return int(math.floor(x / float(BIG_BIN) + 0.5)) * BIG_BIN\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# reference / read loading\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef load_reference(path):\n    data = gzip.open(path, \"rb\").read()\n    lines = data.split(b\"'\"\\\\n\\\")\n    name = lines[0][1:].split()[0].decode()\n    seq = b\\\"\\\".join(l.strip() for l in lines[1:] if l and not l.startswith(b\\\">\\\"))\n    return name, seq.upper().decode()\n\n\n_CODE = np.full(256, 4, dtype=np.uint8)\nfor _i, _c in enumerate(b\\\"ACGT\\\"):\n    _CODE[_c] = _i\n\n\ndef encode(seqbytes: bytes) -> np.ndarray:\n    return _CODE[np.frombuffer(seqbytes, dtype=np.uint8)]\n\n\ndef load_reads(path):\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return (n_records, codes uint8 array of shape (n, READ_LEN)).\\\"\\\"\\\"\n    data = gzip.open(path, \\\"rb\\\").read()\n    lines = data.split(b\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n    seqs = lines[1::4]\n    if seqs and seqs[-1] == b\\\"\\\":\n        seqs = seqs[:-1]\n    seqs = [s for s in seqs if s]\n    n = len(seqs)\n    lens = {len(s) for s in seqs[:2000]}\n    assert lens == {READ_LEN}, f\\\"unexpected read lengths in {path}: {lens}\\\"\n    cat = b\\\"\\\".join(seqs)\n    codes = _CODE[np.frombuffer(cat, dtype=np.uint8)].reshape(n, READ_LEN)\n    return n, codes\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 25-mer index\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef rolling_hash(x: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Hash rows of x (shape (n, w), values 0..4) into uint64 2-bit codes.\n\n    Rows containing a non-ACGT symbol get the sentinel hash UINT64_MAX.\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\n    n = x.shape[0]\n    h = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.uint64)\n    bad = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)\n    for j in range(x.shape[1]):\n        col = x[:, j]\n        h = (h << np.uint64(2)) | (col.astype(np.uint64) & np.uint64(3))\n        bad |= col == 4\n    h[bad] = np.uint64(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\n    return h\n\n\ndef build_index(codes: np.ndarray):\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sorted hashes + positions of every reference K-mer.\\\"\\\"\\\"\n    L = len(codes)\n    n = L - K + 1\n    log(f\\\"building {K}-mer index over {n:,} reference positions ...\\\")\n    t0 = time.time()\n    h = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.uint64)\n    bad = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)\n    for j in range(K):\n        seg = codes[j:j + n]\n        h = (h << np.uint64(2)) | (seg.astype(np.uint64) & np.uint64(3))\n        bad |= seg == 4\n    h[bad] = np.uint64(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\n    order = np.argsort(h, kind=\\\"stable\\\")\n    sorted_hashes = h[order]\n    sorted_pos = order.astype(np.uint32)\n    log(f\\\"index built in {time.time() - t0:.1f}s \\\"\n        f\\\"({int(bad.sum()):,} k-mers masked (N-containing)\\\")\n    return sorted_hashes, sorted_pos\n'@\nSet-Content -LiteralPath output\\\\analysis.py -Value \"'$part1 -Encoding utf8\nWrite-Host \"part1 written: $((Get-Content output'\"\\\\analysis.py).Count) lines\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\chenyuqiong\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command '$part1 = @'\"'\n#\"'!/usr/bin/env python3\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Find a large deletion in shallow paired-end hg38 (chr22-only) WGS data.\n\nInputs (workspace-relative):\n  inputs/find.deletion.r1.fq.gz        mate-1 FASTQ (paired by record order)\n  inputs/find.deletion.r2.fq.gz        mate-2 FASTQ\n  inputs/reference/GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz  GRCh38 chr22 reference, single contig\n\nMethod (pure Python + NumPy; no external aligner needed):\n\n  1. Build an exact 25-mer index of the reference: 2-bit-packed hashes of\n     every reference 25-mer, radix-sorted with NumPy, positions retained.\n  2. Seed-and-verify alignment: for every read, three seeds per strand at\n     offsets 0/62/125. Seed hits are grouped into candidate alignments and\n     each read is classified as uniquely mapped, ambiguously (multi-) mapped,\n     split (chimeric: two distant same-strand loci), or unmapped.\n  3. Three orthogonal deletion signals:\n       a. read depth in 100 kb (reporting) and 10 kb (refinement) bins,\n          normalized by per-bin mappable (ACGT) reference content;\n       b. discordant mate pairs: FR pairs whose mapped span greatly exceeds\n          the insert-size distribution AND whose mates flank the low-depth\n          region -> they bridge the deletion;\n       c. split reads, verified base-by-base against the implied junction\n          (>= 30 bp on each side, crossover consistent with the seeding),\n          giving single-base breakpoint resolution. Split-read junctions are\n          only accepted as evidence when they are consistent with the\n          depth-defined interval (breakpoint within 50 kb of a depth edge\n          and implied size within 20% of the depth interval size).\n  4. Combine evidence: depth defines the deleted interval (primary evidence\n     for position and zygosity); spanning pairs / split reads refine the\n     breakpoints where available. Coordinates are finally rounded to the\n     nearest 100 kb for deletion.tsv (task-mandated reporting precision),\n     while qc.json and report.md keep the refined coordinates plus the\n     per-signal evidence counts, so evidence can be distinguished from\n     precision limits.\n\nOutputs (workspace-relative):\n  output/deletion.tsv  chrom start_100kb end_100kb size_bp supporting_signals\n  output/qc.json       QC metrics + per-signal evidence\n  output/report.md     narrative report\n\nDeterministic; only touches files inside the workspace.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport gzip\nimport json\nimport math\nimport os\nimport time\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nWS = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))  # workspace root\nR1_PATH = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"find.deletion.r1.fq.gz\")\nR2_PATH = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"find.deletion.r2.fq.gz\")\nREF_PATH = os.path.join(WS, \"inputs\", \"reference\", \"GRCh38_chr22.fa.gz\")\nOUT_DIR = os.path.join(WS, \"output\")\n\nK = 25                     # seed length (bp)\nOFFSETS = (0, 62, 125)     # seed offsets inside each 150 bp read\nREAD_LEN = 150\nMAX_SEED_HITS = 16         # seeds hitting more loci are treated as repeats\nBIG_BIN = 100_000          # reporting bin = requested rounding unit\nFINE_BIN = 10_000          # refinement bin for depth boundaries\nMIN_SPLIT_SIDE = 30        # min aligned bp on each side of a junction\nMIN_SPLIT_DEL = 1_000      # split-read implied deletion must be >= this\nEDGE_TOL = 50_000          # split junction may deviate this much from depth edge\n\n# status codes\nUNMAPPED, UNIQUE, AMBIG, SPLIT = 0, 1, 2, 3\n\n\ndef log(msg: str) -> None:\n    print(f\"[analysis] {msg}\", flush=True)\n\n\ndef round_half_up_100kb(x: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Round a coordinate to the nearest 100 kb (half rounds up).\"\"\"\n    return int(math.floor(x / float(BIG_BIN) + 0.5)) * BIG_BIN\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# reference / read loading\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef load_reference(path):\n    data = gzip.open(path, \"rb\").read()\n    lines = data.split(b\"'\"\\\\n\\\")\n    name = lines[0][1:].split()[0].decode()\n    seq = b\\\"\\\".join(l.strip() for l in lines[1:] if l and not l.startswith(b\\\">\\\"))\n    return name, seq.upper().decode()\n\n\n_CODE = np.full(256, 4, dtype=np.uint8)\nfor _i, _c in enumerate(b\\\"ACGT\\\"):\n    _CODE[_c] = _i\n\n\ndef encode(seqbytes: bytes) -> np.ndarray:\n    return _CODE[np.frombuffer(seqbytes, dtype=np.uint8)]\n\n\ndef load_reads(path):\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return (n_records, codes uint8 array of shape (n, READ_LEN)).\\\"\\\"\\\"\n    data = gzip.open(path, \\\"rb\\\").read()\n    lines = data.split(b\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n    seqs = lines[1::4]\n    if seqs and seqs[-1] == b\\\"\\\":\n        seqs = seqs[:-1]\n    seqs = [s for s in seqs if s]\n    n = len(seqs)\n    lens = {len(s) for s in seqs[:2000]}\n    assert lens == {READ_LEN}, f\\\"unexpected read lengths in {path}: {lens}\\\"\n    cat = b\\\"\\\".join(seqs)\n    codes = _CODE[np.frombuffer(cat, dtype=np.uint8)].reshape(n, READ_LEN)\n    return n, codes\n\n\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 25-mer index\n# --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\ndef rolling_hash(x: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Hash rows of x (shape (n, w), values 0..4) into uint64 2-bit codes.\n\n    Rows containing a non-ACGT symbol get the sentinel hash UINT64_MAX.\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\n    n = x.shape[0]\n    h = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.uint64)\n    bad = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)\n    for j in range(x.shape[1]):\n        col = x[:, j]\n        h = (h << np.uint64(2)) | (col.astype(np.uint64) & np.uint64(3))\n        bad |= col == 4\n    h[bad] = np.uint64(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\n    return h\n\n\ndef build_index(codes: np.ndarray):\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Sorted hashes + positions of every reference K-mer.\\\"\\\"\\\"\n    L = len(codes)\n    n = L - K + 1\n    log(f\\\"building {K}-mer index over {n:,} reference positions ...\\\")\n    t0 = time.time()\n    h = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.uint64)\n    bad = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)\n    for j in range(K):\n        seg = codes[j:j + n]\n        h = (h << np.uint64(2)) | (seg.astype(np.uint64) & np.uint64(3))\n        bad |= seg == 4\n    h[bad] = np.uint64(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\n    order = np.argsort(h, kind=\\\"stable\\\")\n    sorted_hashes = h[order]\n    sorted_pos = order.astype(np.uint32)\n    log(f\\\"index built in {time.time() - t0:.1f}s \\\"\n        f\\\"({int(bad.sum()):,} k-mers masked (N-containing)\\\")\n    return sorted_hashes, sorted_pos\n'@\nSet-Content -LiteralPath output\\\\analysis.py -Value \"'$part1 -Encoding utf8\nWrite-Host \"part1 written: $((Get-Content output'\"\\\\analysis.py).Count) lines\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"part1 written: 159 lines\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
