{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_88","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have the full picture:\n\n1. **Main deletion**: chr22:20,000,001-21,000,000 (1-based), a 1,000,000 bp homozygous deletion. Depth is nearly zero across the entire 20.0–21.0 Mb interval. Split reads: 2 reads validate the junction at left breakpoint 20,000,000 (0-based) with a 2 bp crossover window, D = exactly 1,000,000. Discordant pairs: 1 spanning pair with a breakpoint window [19,999,795, 19,999,995] (consistent with ~20.0 Mb given insert-size noise). All three signals agree.\n\n2. **Leading zeros (bins 0-104, 0-10.5 Mb)**: the chr22 short-arm N gap — unmappable, not a deletion. My depth_cn_profile_100kb outputs 0.0 for unmappable bins, which is misleading in the JSON. Should mark unmappable bins as null in the JSON profile.\n\n3. **Bins 128-150 (12.8-15.1 Mb): cn 0.0** — hmm, bins 130-150 are 0.0 and bins 128-129 are 0.78/0.67. Is this region mappable? If it were mappable with CN 0, my \"longest run\" logic should have picked this run (21 bins > 10 bins)! But it picked 200-209. That means bins 130-150 must be unmappable (< 50% valid bp) — probably the centromeric model region on chr22 (GRCh38 chr22 centromere: ~14.9-15.1 Mb? hmm, actually bins 130-150 span 13.0-15.1 Mb). GRCh38 chr22: is the centromere at chr22:13,750,000-15,054,318? Something like that — mostly Ns. So unmappable. OK.\n\n   But wait — bins 128-129 (12.8-13.0 Mb) have cn 0.782/0.67 and appear to be mappable (they were included in the <0.9 list; but were they included in map_big? The <0.9 filter I output includes unmappable ones too). Hmm, these two bins are adjacent to the centromeric N region; probably partially mappable with reduced CN. Why reduced? Reads aligning near the N boundary: my valid_start mask requires a 150bp window to be entirely ACGT — this excludes starts near the N boundary, but the normalization uses valid starts per bin… bins 128-129 might have low valid_start density and the remaining mappable part might be repetitive (pericentromeric satellites), so reads multi-map and get filtered (support < 2 or seeds >16 times). This reduces observed depth. This is a mappability artifact, not a deletion. Fine.\n\n4. **Bins 211-215 (21.1-21.6 Mb): cn 0.886, 0.331, 0.679, 0.806, 0.587** — the region immediately after the deletion. This is the LCR22/segmental duplication region around 21-22 Mb? Actually the known segmental duplications around chr22:20-22 Mb… these reduced CN values are likely due to multi-mapping reads in segmental duplications (reads with seeds hitting >16 loci get excluded). Also bins 158 (0.746), 164-165 (0.85), 187 (0.84), 246 (0.804) — scattered dips = segmental duplication regions (LCR22s at ~16-18 Mb, 21-22 Mb, 24 Mb…). Consistent with chr22 biology. Not a deletion.\n\n   Important check: did my run finder correctly exclude these? Yes — threshold 0.35 and requirement for consecutive runs; bins 212 (0.331) and 215 (0.587)… bin 212 = 0.331 < 0.35! So bin 212 alone would form a run of length 1. Longest-run logic picked the 10-bin run. Good.\n\n   Hmm wait — actually one more consideration: could the deletion extend beyond 21.0 Mb, e.g. could the low CN at 21.1-21.6 be a continuation of heterozygous deletion? No — bin 210 (21.0-21.1) is normal (not in <0.9 list, cn ≈ 1.0), and bin 211 = 0.886 is nearly normal. The 21.15-21.2 Mb dip pattern (0.33-0.68) matches segmental duplications (LCR22A-D is around chr22:21.0-21.5 Mb? Actually LCR22s are in the 17-18.5 Mb region and near 21-22 Mb). The fact that the split reads validate the exact junction at 20,000,000→21,000,000 with D = 1,000,000 settles it: deletion is 20.0-21.0 Mb.\n\n5. **Fine-bin boundaries**: Left edge: 19,990,000-20,000,000 cn 0.869 (last near-normal bin), 20,000,000-20,010,000 cn 0.0 → sharp boundary at 20,000,000. Right edge: 20,990,000-21,000,000 cn 0.012, 21,000,000-21,010,000 cn 0.892 → sharp boundary at 21,000,000. Depth boundaries are at 20,000,000 and 21,000,000 with 10 kb precision; split reads pinpoint the junction at exactly 20,000,000/21,000,000 (first deleted base 1-based = 20,000,001, last = 21,000,000).\n\n   Interesting: the simulated deletion has breakpoints on exact 100 kb grid coordinates.\n\n6. Split cluster \"breakpoint_uncertainty_bp\": 2 — crossover window width is 2 bp across 2 reads (bhi-blo across the cluster). Good.\n\nEverything is consistent. Final answer: chr22:20,000,000-21,000,000, 1 Mb deletion (homozygous), rounded breakpoints 20,000,000 / 21,000,000, size 1,000,000 bp.\n\nNow, improvements before finalizing:\na) In depth_cn_profile_100kb JSON: unmappable bins are null instead of 0.0.\nb) qc.json is currently large-ish but manageable (509 entries). With nulls it will be more accurate.\nc) The TSV's supporting_signals string: looks good. Might tighten the wording. Currently: \"read_depth=10 consecutive 100kb bins at CN 0.00 vs 1.03 genome median (homozygous-like); depth interval chr22:20000001-21000000 | discordant_pairs=1 FR pair(s) spanning the depth region (median span 1,000,563 bp, insert median 500 bp) | split_reads=2 junction read(s) in 1 cluster(s), best at chr22:20000001-21000000 (n=2)\". Good evidence summary.\n\nd) Report: check the current report.md content and confirm it reads well.\n\ne) Also, should re-check the \"spanning_pairs_breakpoint_window_0based\" [19999795, 19999995] vs split breakpoint 20,000,000: 5 bp mismatch because the pair window uses median insert 500 for that pair's true fragment length. Not a problem — documented as fragment-length precision.\n\nf) The depth boundary in qc \"depth_region_1based\": {start: 20000001, end: 21000000}. Wait — depth_start from refine_boundaries = 20,000,000 (0-based start of first reduced fine bin: bin_start 20000000, cn 0.0). 1-based start = 20,000,001. End = 21,000,000 (exclusive 0-based → last deleted base 1-based 21,000,000). Consistent with split reads.\n\ng) One more consideration: the task said \"Round breakpoints to the nearest 100 kb\". My rounding: round