{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_93","type":"reasoning","text":"Whoa — the mismatch rate for reads mapped with support==3 is 36.7%?! And it's bimodal: about half the reads have 0 mismatches, half have ≥5 mismatches. Reads with ≥5 mismatches are mapped to wrong loci (mis-mapping) — a seed hit somewhere in a repeat by chance... no wait, support==3 means all three plus-strand seeds agree on the same position. Hmm, but if all three seeds agree on the same position, the read should match at that position... unless the read is a reverse-strand read being forced onto the plus strand? No — the strand is determined by which strand's seeds agree.\n\nWait, actually — hmm, 9,842 out of 20,000 reads have ≥5 mismatches against the reference at the reported position, yet all three seeds agree there. That's weird. Could it be... the reference has N's, and my mismatch counting treats N(4) != base as a mismatch! Reads overlapping low-mappability/repetitive regions... no wait, reads mapped with three consistent seeds shouldn't overlap N's.\n\nHold on — another possibility: paralogous sequence variants. Reads originating from a duplicated region (e.g., LCR22) that isn't present in the reference, or reads from a region with an insertion in the reference relative to the sample... hmm.\n\nWait wait. Let me rethink: maybe these are reads from the deleted allele! For a homozygous deletion, no reads should come from the deleted region...\n\nHmm, let me think about what causes the bimodal distribution: 10,155 reads with perfect matches, 9,842 with ≥5 mismatches. Could the latter be reads where the sequencing simulation added errors?? But then the mismatches should be distributed across all reads, not be bimodal.\n\nAnother thought: these reads might be mapped to the wrong copy of a segmental duplication — i.e., the reads originate from copy B, but due to shared 25-mers they got mapped to copy A (the seeds hit both copies, but one copy has fewer hits...). With a MAX_SEED_HITS=16 cap