pping.\n\nOK so the deletion call is robust. The bimodal mismatch finding only affects the QC description: of the mapped reads, about half of the support==3 set is actually paralogous mis-mappings. Hmm wait, actually — hold on. Let me re-verify: maybe reads with ≥5 mismatches are... Let me look at where those reads map. If they cluster in the 16-18Mb, 21-22.5Mb, and 24Mb regions (segmental duplications), that confirms it. Let me quickly check the positions of high-mismatch reads.\n\nActually, this matters for one specific reason: it could inflate depth in some bins, but not create false depth drops. The deletion call stands on: (a) 10 consecutive 100kb bins with CN~0, (b) 2 split reads validating the exact 1Mb junction, (c) 1 discordant pair spanning it. Solid.\n\nBut wait — one more check on the split reads themselves: could the 2 junction reads also be mis-mappings? They're validated base-by-base across the full 150bp against the junction sequence (≥30bp on each side with ≤2bp crossover window) — random matches are effectively impossible. So no.\n\nNow, about \"distinguishing evidence from precision limits\" — good material for the report:\n- Depth evidence is primary; breakpoints refined to 10kb bins by depth alone.\n- 2 split reads + 1 discordant pair confirm at base resolution: breakpoints exactly at 20,000,000/21,000,000.\n- Rounded to 100kb grid: 20,000,000 / 21,000,000.\n\nOne nagging question remains: why do about half the reads have so many mismatches? Let me quickly check the genomic distribution of high-mismatch reads. If they concentrate in known segmental duplication regions, I'll note in the report that reads from duplicated regions were conservatively handled (excluded from split evidence, and depth was normalized per mappable bin).\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Another hypothesis for the bimodality: the sample is not pure reference — the reads were simulated from a genome that contains the 1Mb deletion and possibly other variants (SNPs/indels). Reads overlapping heterozygous SNPs would show isolated mismatches (1-2), not ≥5. The ≥5 class with support-3 mapping strongly suggests paralogous mis-mapping. Let me verify the positional clustering."}}
