{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_96","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting! Reads with high mismatch are uniformly distributed across the entire chromosome (about 300/Mb everywhere), not localized to segmental duplications. Perfect reads are also uniform. In other words, about 50% of the support==3 reads have ≥5 mismatches, and they're everywhere.\n\nThis pattern — a genome-wide bimodal distribution of 0 mismatches vs ≥5 mismatches — suggests that the simulation generated reads from two haplotypes: one identical to the reference, and the other highly diverged? Or about half the reads contain systematic sequencing errors? Hmm, ≥5 mismatches per 150bp = 3.3% divergence — too high for human heterozygosity (0.1%), but reasonable as an intentionally diverged \"alternative haplotype\" in a simulation... hmm.\n\nWait, actually — another thought: these might be reads sampled from the deleted chromosome... no, homozygous deletions don't produce reads.\n\nHold on, let me reconsider: could my alignment be systematically assigning reads to a wrong position that is 1-2bp offset? No — if that were the case, we'd see about 1 mismatch at most, or a specific pattern; and the seeds would have to agree exactly.\n\nHmm, how can a read have three seeds (75bp total) that exactly match the reference at some locus, while the full 150bp has ≥5 mismatches? The mismatches must lie outside the three seed regions (offsets 0-25, 62-87, 125-150). Gaps: 25-62 (37bp) and 87-125 (38bp) — 75bp of unseeded sequence. If errors concentrate there... but if errors are random, seeds should take damage too (P(seed intact) = (1-e)^25).\n\nHmm, wait, let me actually reconsider the numbers: seeds with no hit = 848,903/1,992,738 = 42.6%. If errors were random with per-base rate e: P(seed hit) = (1-e)^25 = 0.574 → e = 2.2%. In that case P(read has 0 errors) = (1-e)^150 = 0.035 — but we observed 50% of reads with 0 mismatches! Contradiction. Therefore the errors are not random and uniform: reads are bimodal — about 50% are clean, and about 50% carry many differences.\n\nHmm... could the simulation have included an error profile where some reads are noisy (e.g., 10% of reads at high error rate)? If 50% of reads have about 10 mismatches each... the no-hit seed fraction: clean reads contribute 0 no-hit seeds; noisy reads (with about 10 errors distributed over 150bp): P(seed with no errors) = ... 10 errors distributed randomly over 150bp, the expected number of errors per seed (25bp) = 10/6 = 1.67 → P(seed clean) ≈ e^-1.67 ≈ 0.19. No-hit fraction = 0.5×(1-0.19) + ... hmm, = 0.5×0.81 = 0.405 ≈ 42.6% observed ✓. And the bimodal mismatch histogram ✓ (noisy reads about 10 mismatches, ≥5 in histogram bucket).\n\nSo about half the reads are noisy (average ~10 mismatches ≈ Q11?) — an intentionally adversarial benchmark mixture, probably to test robustness. My ≥2-seed alignment can still place them (each noisy read retains ~0.5 clean seeds on average per strand × 3 seeds...).\n\nHmm wait, but then: noisy reads that map with support 3 — all three seeds clean, but mismatches between seeds — are correctly mapped (the sequences between seeds may differ from the reference due to errors, but the position is correct). That's fine for depth counting.\n\nBut the critical question: does this affect the deletion call? Inside 20-21Mb, only 6 reads mapped (out of a 20k sample → about 20 total; CN 0.002 ✓ nearly zero). A homozygous deletion can't be filled in by noisy reads because reads from the deleted region don't exist.\n\nCould noisy reads create a false depth dip somewhere? They reduce mappability (support<2 → excluded), so depth in noisy read-rich regions... noise is read-level, not region-level, so no spatial bias. No issue.\n\nCould a false split read arise from a noisy read? Split verification requires a base-level match of ≥30bp on both sides of the junction — noisy reads typically wouldn't pass; the consistency filter also requires junction coordinates to match the depth edges. Only 2 passed, both exactly at 20,000,000/21,000,000, D=1,000,000. ✓.\n\nOK — deletion call confirmed. Next, update the analysis to also log/record this read quality bimodality in QC (it's good for explaining the 42% seed no-hit statistic and shows thoroughness). Add a small post-alignment QC computation in main(): on a subsample of mapped reads (e.g., 20k), compute the mismatch distribution against the primary alignment, and record clean_fraction and mean mismatches of noisy reads. Also make unmappable bins null in the depth profile.\n\nAlso, regarding mismatch counting for QC: to avoid overhangs, count only reads within [0, L-READ_LEN] (already filtered).\n\nImplementation plan (edits to analysis.py):\n1. depth_profile already returns mappable_big (in main I had dropped it from the unpacking — check: `cn_fine, cn_big, map_fine, map_big = depth_profile(...)` ✓ it's returned).\n2. In qc construction: `\"depth_cn_profile_100kb\": [round(float(x),3) if map_big[i] else None for i,x in enumerate(cn_big)]`.\n3. Add read-level mismatch QC: after alignment, subsample usable reads (both mates), compute mismatches against refc, and classify as clean (≤2 mismatches) vs noisy. Record: subsample size, clean fraction, mean mismatches of noisy reads. Runtime cost: 20k reads × 150 comparisons = trivial.\n\n   Code:\n   ```python\n   # read-level mismatch QC on a subsample of placed reads\n   sub = np.random.default_rng(0)  # deterministic\n   ```\n   Hmm, keep it deterministic without random: take the first 20000 usable R1 reads.\n   ```python\n   idx_q = np.nonzero(usable1)[0][:20000]\n   pos_q = a1[\"start\"][idx_q]\n   mm = np.array([int((c1[i] != refc[p:p+READ_LEN]).sum()) for i, p in zip(idx_q, pos_q)])\n   clean_frac = float((mm <= 2).mean())\n   noisy_mean = float(mm[mm > 2].mean()) if (mm > 2).any() else 0.0\n   ```\n   Python loop 20k × slice comparison — ~1-2 seconds. OK.\n   Add to qc[\"reads\"]: \"qc_subsample\": {\"n\":…, \"clean_reads_frac\":…, \"noisy_reads_mean_mismatches\":…, \"note\": \"bimodal: …\"}.\n\n4. Report: add a sentence about the read population being bimodal (~X% clean / ~Y% noisy with ~Z mismatches) and its implications (alignment robust via ≥2-seed requirement; depth unaffected because noise is not region-specific). Also note that unmappable bins (N gap / centromere) show as null in the profile and are excluded from calling.\n\nAlso, fix the wording in the report's \"Signals\" section, and maybe mention the 6 reads inside the deletion (residual mismapped reads, CN 0.002).\n\nAdditionally, one more check about TSV rounding: \"round breakpoints to nearest 100 kb\" — refined start 20,000,001 → 20,000,000 ✓; end 21,000,000 → 21,000,000 ✓. size_bp = 1,000,000 ✓.\n\nOK, let me apply these edits. I'll do it with a small python patch script.\n\nEdit 1: qc depth profile line:\n```python\n            \"depth_cn_profile_100kb\": [round(float(x), 3) for x in cn_big],\n```\n→\n```python\n            \"depth_cn_profile_100kb\": [\n                round(float(x), 3) if map_big[i] else None\n                for i, x in enumerate(cn_big)],\n```\n\nEdit 2: insert mismatch QC before the \"# ---------------- outputs\" section... actually simpler: compute right after the alignment rate log line. Insert after the `log(f\"alignment: ...\")` block:\n\n```python\n    # read-level mismatch QC (subsample): detects noisy read subpopulation\n    idx_q = np.nonzero(usable1)[0][:20000]\n    pos_q = a1[\"start\"][idx_q]\n    mm = np.array([int((c1[i] != refc[p:p + READ_LEN]).sum())\n                   for i, p in zip(idx_q, pos_q)])\n    clean_frac = float((mm <= 2).mean())\n    noisy = mm[mm > 2]\n    noisy_mean = float(noisy.mean()) if noisy.size else 0.0\n    log(f\"read QC subsample ({len(idx_q):,}): {clean_frac:.1%} clean reads, \"\n        f\"noisy reads carry ~{noisy_mean:.1f} mismatches\")\n```\n\nThen in qc[\"reads\"] add:\n```python\n            \"qc_subsample_mapped_reads\": {\n                \"n\": int(len(idx_q)),\n                \"clean_reads_fraction_le2mm\": round(clean_frac, 4),\n                \"noisy_reads_mean_mismatches\": round(noisy_mean, 2),\n                \"note\": (\"bimodal read population; noisy reads are spread \"\n                         \"genome-wide and do not bias regional depth\")},\n```\n\nEdit 3: report — update the Data section to include the read QC line. The build_report function gets qc; add a bullet point.\n\nLet me write the patch."}}
